---
/**
 * Privacy Policy — a notice describing this site's actual data practices,
 * not legal advice given to the visitor. Internal notes on legal review,
 * governing-law language, and entity status live in
 * docs/content-rights-checklist.md, not on this public page.
 *
 * Identifies the operator as "The Electric Lavender Train" only — no LLC,
 * corporate, or registered-entity status is claimed anywhere on this page,
 * because nothing in this repository verifies one.
 *
 * Several sections below describe features that are configured but not yet
 * turned on (the Brevo-hosted newsletter, the Google Form for event-media
 * submissions) using the same conditional, "once configured" language the
 * site itself uses (`Newsletter.astro`, `EventMediaSubmission.astro`) —
 * never as settled, currently operating practices.
 */

import LegalPage from "../layouts/LegalPage.astro";
import siteConfig, { formatPageTitle } from "../data/siteConfig";

const title = formatPageTitle("Privacy Policy");
const description = `How ${siteConfig.bandNameFormal} collects, uses, and protects information submitted through this website.`;
const email = siteConfig.contact.email;
---

<LegalPage
  title={title}
  description={description}
  kicker="Legal"
  heading="Privacy Policy"
  effectiveDate="August 20, 2026"
>
  <p>
    This website is operated by The Electric Lavender Train (“we,” “us,” or “our”). This Privacy
    Policy explains what information we collect through this website, why we collect it, who we
    share it with, and the choices you have.
  </p>

  <h2>Information you provide to us</h2>
  <p>
    This site has four inquiry forms on the <a href={siteConfig.routes.contactBooking}>Contact
    & Booking</a> page: <strong>Booking Inquiry</strong>, <strong>Merch Inquiry</strong>,
    <strong>Other</strong>, and <strong>Content Removal Request</strong>. What you submit depends
    on which form you use:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Booking Inquiry:</strong> name and email (required); phone, organization or venue,
      preferred date, estimated audience size, budget, and special song requests (all optional —
      entering a budget never implies a guaranteed price, availability, or accepted booking, and a
      song request is never a guarantee it will be played); event type and event location
      (required — location asks only for “city and venue, if known,” not a street address); event
      details (required).
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Merch Inquiry:</strong> name, email, and the merchandise item you're asking about
      (required); quantity/size/other details (optional); your message (required).
    </li>
    <li><strong>Other:</strong> name, email, and subject (required); your message (required).</li>
    <li>
      <strong>Content Removal Request:</strong> name and email (required); the URL or location of
      the content, a description of it, your reason for the request or relationship to the
      content, and the action you're requesting (required). This form has no file upload — describe
      or link to the content in text. Submitting it starts our review of your request; it does not
      guarantee that the content will be removed.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Every field on these four forms is plain text — none of them accepts a file upload. (Separately,
    if you choose to submit event photos or video through the Google Form described under “Event
    media submissions” below, that upload happens entirely on Google's own site, not this one.) We
    ask that you not include payment card numbers, government identification numbers, or medical
    information in any of these forms — a reminder to that effect appears directly above the forms
    themselves.
  </p>
  <p>
    If you email us directly at <a href={`mailto:${email}`}>{email}</a> instead of using a form,
    that message and whatever you choose to include in it is handled the same way any email is
    handled by Gmail/Google, described further below.
  </p>

  <h2>Information collected automatically</h2>
  <p>
    When you submit one of the forms above, our form-delivery provider, <strong>Formspree</strong>,
    may automatically receive standard technical information along with your submission — such as
    your IP address, browser and device information, the time of submission, and the page that
    referred you to the form. This is typical of how web forms function and is not something we
    configure separately; it comes from Formspree's own processing of the request. We do not use
    this technical information to build an advertising profile of you.
  </p>
  <p>
    This website's hosting provider (expected to be Netlify, though the final choice of host may
    change before launch) may also log standard web-server access information — such as IP
    address, requested pages, and timestamps — as part of ordinary hosting operations, independent
    of anything you submit through a form.
  </p>

  <h2>How we use your information</h2>
  <p>We use the information you submit to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Respond to booking, merchandise, and general inquiries;</li>
    <li>Review and respond to content removal requests;</li>
    <li>Communicate with you about an inquiry or, where applicable, a confirmed booking;</li>
    <li>Identify and prevent spam and abusive submissions; and</li>
    <li>Maintain records reasonably necessary for our business, accounting, or legal purposes.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    We do <strong>not</strong> sell your information, and we do not use inquiry information for
    behavioral advertising.
  </p>

  <h2>Service providers we use</h2>
  <p>The following third-party services are involved in operating this website and handling inquiries:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Formspree</strong> — receives, processes, and may store the contents of form submissions, and delivers them to us by email. Formspree has its own privacy practices, which you can review at their website.</li>
    <li><strong>Gmail / Google</strong> — the email service where inquiry notifications and any direct emails to <a href={`mailto:${email}`}>{email}</a> are received and stored.</li>
    <li><strong>Brevo</strong> — our approved newsletter provider. The hosted signup page and its double opt-in confirmation are configured but not yet live; once they are, Brevo hosts that page, collects your signup, and sends the newsletter. See “Newsletter signup” below.</li>
    <li><strong>Google Forms / Google Drive</strong> — our approved provider for the event-media submission feature described below, pending creation of the actual Google Form. Once that feature is turned on, Google Forms is the external page that collects those submissions, and files/responses are stored in Google's systems (typically Drive), not ours.</li>
    <li><strong>Our website host</strong> — serves the website itself and may log standard access information as described above.</li>
    <li><strong>Sanity</strong> — the content platform used to manage and publish this website's public editorial content (such as band photos, event listings, music releases, and merchandise descriptions). Sanity does not receive or store anything you submit through the contact forms.</li>
    <li><strong>YouTube</strong> — used to embed video content on this site, including the Home and Music pages' featured videos and the Gallery &amp; Merchandise page's gallery videos. See “Embedded YouTube videos” below.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Newsletter signup</h2>
  <p>
    This site has a separate, optional newsletter signup, distinct from the four inquiry forms
    above — submitting an inquiry never automatically subscribes you to anything. We are planning
    to use <strong>Brevo</strong>'s own hosted signup page for this: clicking the newsletter link on
    this site takes you to a page hosted directly by Brevo (not this website), where you enter your
    own information. Until that hosted page is actually configured, the newsletter link on this
    site shows a clear “not yet available” notice instead of a working signup.
  </p>
  <p>Once live, we intend for that signup to work as follows, though the exact fields depend on how the Brevo form is ultimately configured:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Signup uses <strong>double opt-in</strong> — you'll receive a confirmation email and must confirm before you're actually subscribed.</li>
    <li>Email address is required; a name field, if the form includes one, is optional.</li>
    <li>You'll be able to choose between our regular weekly newsletter and a separate, optional new-show-alert preference — choosing one does not automatically opt you into the other.</li>
    <li>Every newsletter email will include an unsubscribe link, and Brevo's hosted preferences page (if enabled) lets you change what you're subscribed to without unsubscribing entirely.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Brevo is a third-party email marketing platform with its own privacy practices, and its hosted
    signup/preferences pages and outgoing emails may involve their own tracking (such as email-open
    or link-click measurement, which is common to most email marketing tools) that this document
    cannot fully describe until we've configured and reviewed the live Brevo account ourselves. We
    do not claim that tracking is disabled on the Brevo side — if you have questions about what
    Brevo itself collects, its own privacy policy is the authoritative source, and we will update
    this section once our production Brevo configuration has been verified.
  </p>

  <h2>Event media submissions</h2>
  <p>
    The Gallery &amp; Merchandise page has an approved, planned “Share Your Event Media” section,
    which — once an editor turns it on <em>and</em> a real Google Form has been configured — links
    out to a <strong>Google Form</strong>. This site never hosts that upload itself: the form, and
    any files you submit through it, live entirely on Google's systems. This feature is fail-closed
    while it isn't fully ready: if the section isn't turned on at all, it doesn't appear anywhere on
    the page; if it's turned on but no Google Form has been configured yet, the section appears with
    a plain notice instead of a working link. Either way, nothing described below is actually
    happening until a real, working link is live, and we do not claim the consent/license mechanism
    described below is operational until it is.
  </p>
  <p>Once the Google Form is live, submitting through it is expected to involve:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Signing in with a Google account, since Google Forms requires this to accept file uploads;</li>
    <li>Selecting which ELT event your photos or video are from;</li>
    <li>Uploading the photo/video files themselves;</li>
    <li>Google associating your submission with information tied to that Google account (such as your name and email address), the same way any Google Form does; and</li>
    <li>Agreeing to the specific consent/license language that will be presented on the Google Form itself — this policy summarizes our intended approach, but the form's own wording is authoritative for what you're agreeing to.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    That submission is processed and stored by Google (typically in Google Drive), governed by
    Google's own privacy policy and terms — not this one. Our intended handling, once this feature
    is live, distinguishes what happens to your upload depending on whether we use it:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>If we don't select your submission for use:</strong> the original upload is targeted for deletion within approximately one month.</li>
    <li><strong>If we do select your submission for use:</strong> the media is transferred out of the Google Form/Drive into an ELT-controlled media library, and the record of your consent/permission is retained for as long as reasonably necessary to support that authorized use and to document that permission was given.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    <strong>The exact retention schedule and final wording of this section remain pending Hunter's
    (the band's) approval and licensed-attorney review</strong> — this describes our intended
    approach, not a finalized policy. Submitting media does not guarantee it will be published
    anywhere, and a submission that includes other identifiable people — or any minor — may need
    additional permission before we use it; see “Third-party links and embedded services” and our
    Terms of Use for how we handle that. If you'd like something you submitted removed, use the
    Content Removal Request form above.
  </p>

  <h2>Analytics and advertising</h2>
  <p>
    At launch, this website does not use Google Analytics, Meta Pixel, or any other first-party
    analytics, advertising, or remarketing tracking technology of our own. If that changes in the
    future, this Privacy Policy — and, where required, our approach to cookie consent — will be
    updated first. This is separate from what Brevo's or Google's own hosted pages may do on their
    own domains, described above.
  </p>

  <h2>Cookies and similar technologies</h2>
  <p>
    We do not configure analytics cookies, behavioral-advertising cookies, or remarketing trackers of
    any kind on this website ourselves. We are not able to promise that no cookie or similar local
    storage will ever be set by any part of this site or the third-party services it links to or
    embeds — for example, YouTube may set its own cookies once you choose to activate an embedded
    video (see below), and Brevo's or Google's hosted pages may set their own cookies once you visit
    them — but nothing along those lines is configured by us, and we do not use cookies to build an
    advertising profile of visitors.
  </p>

  <h2>Embedded YouTube videos</h2>
  <p>
    YouTube videos on this site — including the Home and Music pages' featured videos and the
    videos in the Gallery &amp; Merchandise page's photo/video gallery — are embedded using
    YouTube's privacy-enhanced mode (<code>youtube-nocookie.com</code>), and the video player itself
    is never loaded until you actively click to play it — simply visiting a page with an embedded
    video does not load anything from YouTube. Once you choose to play a video, you're interacting
    directly with YouTube's own player, which YouTube (owned by Google) operates according to its
    own privacy policy and terms. YouTube may show advertising within the embedded player. YouTube
    states that advertising shown through privacy-enhanced mode is non-personalized — we report that
    as YouTube's own stated behavior, not as our own guarantee of how Google's systems actually
    operate. YouTube still processes information about your interaction with the activated player
    according to its own practices, which we do not control or fully disclose. To be clear, this is
    entirely separate from the "no analytics, behavioral advertising, or remarketing trackers"
    statement above, which describes what <em>we</em> configure on this website — it does not, and
    could not, override how YouTube's own embedded product behaves once activated.
  </p>

  <h2>Data retention</h2>
  <p>
    We do not keep inquiry information forever, but we also do not commit here to one fixed
    calendar deadline for every submission — how long a given record needs to be kept genuinely
    depends on what it is:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Ordinary Booking, Merch, and Other inquiries</strong> (Formspree and Gmail) that
      never turn into anything further: retained only as reasonably necessary to respond to them
      and to maintain appropriate business records — not indefinitely. Closed inquiries like these
      are periodically reviewed, and deleted once we no longer have a reasonable business need to
      keep them.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Inquiries connected to an active negotiation, a confirmed booking, a contract, a
      payment, a dispute, or a legal obligation</strong> are retained for as long as reasonably
      necessary to manage that engagement, maintain the corresponding business/financial/accounting
      records, resolve the dispute, or satisfy the legal obligation — which may be considerably
      longer than an ordinary, closed inquiry.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Content Removal Requests</strong> (Formspree and Gmail): retained long enough to
      complete our review, document the outcome, and demonstrate that the request was addressed;
      after that, handled under the same reasonable-necessity, periodic-review approach as other
      closed inquiries above.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Gmail records generally:</strong> notifications and any direct correspondence sent to
      <a href={`mailto:${email}`}>{email}</a> are retained in that account under the same
      criteria described above, subject to Google's own account-level backup/retention practices,
      which are outside our direct control.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Newsletter records</strong> (Brevo, once live): retained by Brevo for as long as you
      remain subscribed, plus a suppression-list record after you unsubscribe (so we don't
      accidentally re-add you) — the exact suppression-record retention period is set by Brevo's own
      platform defaults and has not yet been finalized on our end.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Event-media uploads</strong> (Google Form/Drive, once live): an unselected original
      upload is targeted for deletion within approximately one month; media we select for use, and
      the record of your consent to that use, are retained for as long as reasonably necessary to
      support that use. See “Event media submissions” above.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Review and deletion under this policy is a manual, periodic human process (not an automated
    one) — we do not currently run, and do not claim to run, any automatic deletion of Formspree or
    Gmail records. <strong>The exact final wording of this section, and any specific retention
    schedule, remain pending Hunter's (the band's) approval and licensed-attorney review.</strong>
  </p>

  <h2>Your rights and choices</h2>
  <p>
    You may request access to, correction of, or deletion of information you've submitted to us by
    emailing <a href={`mailto:${email}`}>{email}</a>, or — for content you'd like taken down — using
    the Content Removal Request form. What we can do depends on where the information lives:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Submissions stored in our Formspree account</strong> and <strong>notifications and
      conversations in our Gmail account</strong> are ours to locate and delete, and we will do so
      for a valid request (subject to the retention exceptions above — e.g. records tied to a
      confirmed booking).
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Newsletter preferences:</strong> once Brevo is live, you'll be able to unsubscribe or
      change your weekly-newsletter / new-show-alert preferences directly through links in the email
      or Brevo's own hosted preferences page, without contacting us — though you're also welcome to
      email us.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Content removal requests and event-media uploads:</strong> submitting a Content
      Removal Request starts our review of the specific content you identify; it does not
      automatically guarantee removal, and we'll follow up by email. If you submitted event media
      through the Google Form and want it removed, use the Content Removal Request form and
      describe what you submitted.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Any spreadsheet or other export</strong> we've made from the above is likewise ours to
      delete, and is held to the same retention rules as the original.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Provider-controlled security logs, records we're legally required to keep, and
      backups</strong> our providers retain as part of their own disaster-recovery practices are
      outside our direct control — we can ask our providers to remove them, but cannot guarantee
      the exact timing of that deletion.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Security</h2>
  <p>
    We take reasonable steps to protect information submitted through this website, including
    relying on our providers' own security practices (such as Formspree's, Brevo's, and Google's).
    No method of transmission or storage over the internet is completely secure, and we cannot
    guarantee absolute security.
  </p>

  <h2>Children's privacy</h2>
  <p>
    This website and its forms are not directed to children under 13, and we do not knowingly
    collect personal information from children under 13. This includes event-media submissions:
    please do not submit photos or video whose primary subject is a child, and if a submission
    includes a child incidentally, we handle that cautiously and may decline to publish it. If you
    believe a child has submitted information to us, or appears in submitted media, please contact
    us at <a href={`mailto:${email}`}>{email}</a> so we can address it.
  </p>

  <h2>California privacy disclosures</h2>
  <p>
    For California residents: the categories of personal information we collect are limited to what's
    described above — identifiers (name, email, phone if provided), the content of your inquiry or
    removal request, newsletter subscription/preference data (once Brevo is live), and, for anyone
    who uses the event-media submission feature, the photo/video files and associated Google-account
    information described above. We do not sell this information, and we share it only with the
    service providers listed above, for the purposes described above — not for cross-context
    behavioral advertising. This website does not currently respond differently to browser "Do Not
    Track" signals or other cross-site tracking opt-out signals, because it does not engage in
    cross-site tracking or behavioral advertising in the first place. <strong>Whether the California
    Consumer Privacy Act (CCPA) or other state privacy laws apply to this site, and the exact wording
    this section should ultimately use, has not been reviewed by an attorney</strong> — this section
    describes our practices, not a legal conclusion about which laws govern them. We will note the
    date of any material change to this policy at the top of this page.
  </p>

  <h2>International visitors</h2>
  <p>
    This website is operated from, and primarily intended for visitors in, the United States. If
    you access this site from outside the United States, your information may be transferred to
    and processed in the United States or other countries where our service providers operate,
    which may have different data protection laws than your home country.
  </p>

  <h2>Changes to this policy</h2>
  <p>
    We may update this Privacy Policy from time to time. Material changes will be reflected by
    updating the effective date at the top of this page. We encourage you to review this page
    periodically.
  </p>

  <h2>Contact us</h2>
  <p>
    Questions about this Privacy Policy, or requests regarding your information, can be sent to <a href={`mailto:${email}`}>{email}</a>.
  </p>
</LegalPage>
